<% content_for :title, "Your collection — Tondo" %>
<% content_for :description, "The works you have kept on Tondo." %>

<%= render "shared/masthead", label: "Your collection", here: :collection,
      aside: pluralize(@rows.size, "work") %>

<main class="page <%= "page--empty" if @rows.empty? %>">
  <% if @rows.empty? %>
    <div class="ornament" aria-hidden="true">✦</div>
    <p class="coda__line">The works you keep will gather here.</p>
    <%# This sentence used to read "Keep this sits under every artwork", naming a
        label that no longer exists — the control is a bookmark glyph now
        (`decisions/0010`). With the labels gone this is the only place in the
        product that can tell a first-time reader what that mark does, so it names
        the shape rather than describing an action. %>
    <% if current_device&.claimed_at %>
      <%# The state this whole story's copy effort exists for (story 0017
          Release 2, design review 3A). A reader signed in, their works moved to
          the account, they signed out, and this page is now empty. The line
          above is written for someone who has never kept anything, and to this
          reader it reads as loss — thirty seconds after a dialog told them
          nothing would be deleted.

          `claimed_at` is the only way to tell the two apart: same empty
          collection, same device, entirely different sentence owed. %>
      <p class="coda__note">Your kept works are with your account. Sign in and
        they come back — nothing was deleted.</p>
    <% else %>
      <p class="coda__note">The bookmark under each artwork keeps it here.</p>
    <% end %>
    <%# ISSUE-001 was this page offering only `Today →`, stranding a reader who
        arrived with nothing kept. The compass answers it now, and answers it on
        every screen rather than one at a time. %>
  <% else %>
    <%# Their order, newest kept first — not ours. No month headings: "the month
        I kept it" is not a fact anybody wants. Every row links (story 0030): a
        Daily Pick's dated page when it has one, the work's own permanent page
        when it does not — never a day the curator removed and never a work
        that was never picked at all; the two are indistinguishable and both
        get the same fallback. %>
    <ol class="days">
      <% @rows.each_with_index do |(painting, pick), i| %>
        <%= render "days/row", painting: painting, pick: pick,
              current: @current, eager: i < 4 %>
      <% end %>
    </ol>

    <footer class="coda">
      <div class="ornament" aria-hidden="true">✦</div>
      <p class="coda__line">To let one go, open it and unkeep it.</p>
      <%# Said once, quietly, before it can be discovered the hard way. A
          device's keeps live against this device; an account's live with the
          account. Stating the limit up front is what keeps it from reading as
          a betrayal later — see specs/personas.md, persona 6. %>
      <% if current_user %>
        <p class="coda__note">Kept with your account — free, and yours.</p>
      <% else %>
        <p class="coda__note">Kept on this device — free, and yours.</p>
      <% end %>
    </footer>
  <% end %>

  <%# The signpost, not the doors (story 0017).

      Both delete buttons used to live here, and story 0016 put the device one
      here in a hurry because the filed privacy policy promised in-app deletion
      the majority reader did not have. They moved to `/you` so that a
      destructive control has one home, one confirm string and one tested path.

      What stays is a word. The corner's mark rides the bar on every screen, but
      a ring and a dot are not a label, and the default iOS reader — a
      registered device that never signed in — would otherwise have swapped a
      button that says what it does for a glyph that does not. This is the room
      the policy names, so this is where the path to the exit gets named too.

      Outside the empty-state branch, for the reason the buttons were: a reader
      with nothing kept still owns their own door. %>
  <% if current_user %>
    <footer class="account">
      <p class="coda__note">Signed in with <%= current_user.signed_in_summary %>.</p>
      <%= link_to "Your corner", corner_path, class: "caps-link" %>
    </footer>
  <% elsif current_device %>
    <footer class="account">
      <p class="coda__note">Kept on this device, with no account.</p>
      <%= link_to "Your corner", corner_path, class: "caps-link" %>
    </footer>
  <% end %>

  <%= render "shared/legal" %>
</main>
